1. A computer-implemented method for dispatching a plurality of buildings during a demand response event, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, energy consumption data for each building of the plurality of buildings, the energy consumption data including time-series energy consumption values;

generating, by the one or more processors, lagged energy consumption data for each building by shifting at least a portion of the time-series energy consumption values by one or more lag intervals;

receiving, by the one or more processors, outside temperature data corresponding to the time-series energy consumption values;

for each building of the plurality of buildings, fitting a nonlinear parabolic energy response model that relates energy consumption to at least the outside temperature data and the lagged energy consumption data;

determining, for each building, model residuals between energy consumption values predicted by the nonlinear parabolic energy response model and corresponding measured energy consumption values;

minimizing the model residuals to estimate model parameters for each building;

determining, for each building, an energy lag metric based at least in part on the lagged energy consumption data and the estimated model parameters, the energy lag metric indicating a delayed energy response of the building;

prioritizing the plurality of buildings for dispatch during the demand response event according to the energy lag metric; and

dispatching one or more buildings of the plurality of buildings in accordance with the prioritization to reduce or shift energy consumption during the demand response event.

1. A computer-implemented method for managing energy demand across a plurality of buildings, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, for each building of the plurality of buildings, time-series data comprising energy consumption data, time data, and outside temperature data;

estimating, by the one or more processors, for each building, a thermal or energy lag parameter based at least in part on a temporal relationship among the energy consumption data, the time data, and the outside temperature data, wherein the thermal or energy lag parameter represents a delay between a change in outside temperature and a corresponding change in energy consumption of the building;

generating, by the one or more processors, a demand response priority value for each building based at least in part on the estimated thermal or energy lag parameter for the building;

creating, by the one or more processors, a demand response dispatch order for the plurality of buildings by ranking the buildings according to the demand response priority values; and

transmitting, by the one or more processors, one or more demand response control instructions to at least one building energy system in accordance with the demand response dispatch order.
